#edb/ref 1069 "q-171332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060112 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the contrapositive of the following statement:\n\n**If** ${1, 2}∩{2, 3} = ∅$, **then** $6$ is prime." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060112 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38ac2a48-89e3-5cdd-bcff-f22b6ccd3098" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060113 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477559 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186060113 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477560 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186060113 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477561 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186060113 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171334" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060113 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060113 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ **If** $10\\ge 1,$ **then** $3\\in R.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n- **I.** If $10<1,$ then $3\\notin R.$\n- **II.** If $3\\notin R,$ then $10<1.$\n- **III.** If $3\\in R,$ then $10\\ge 1.$\n\nCompare the logical forms for arbitrary truth values of the antecedent and consequent.\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186060113 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37ad276a-88ce-596c-9a24-406a32aa6e25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060113 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other, but not to the original statement.\n\n![](images/d9/d946d2a81d46cbde934ebbab54f9b69b514092e02da4f8a3b79768f218929449.png)\n\nSo, we are looking for the contrapositive of the given statement.\n\n- Statement I is the inverse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement II is the contrapositive. $\\,✓$\n\n- Statement III is the converse, not the contrapositive. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060413 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186059100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060413 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81982" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060413 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the following statement:\n\n**If** my pet is not a fish, **then** it needs a food bowl." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060413 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd1cf6c1-4b1f-5501-a4e7-c6fd63dc9d14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060415 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186059112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060415 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060415 #edb/ref 1071 "**If** my pet is not a fish, **then** it needs a food bowl.\n\nWhich of the following statements are equivalent to the conditional statement above?\n\n1. If my pet does not need a food bowl, then it is a fish.\n2. If my pet needs a food bowl, then it is not a fish.\n3. If my pet is a fish, then it does not need a food bowl." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060415 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13793e90-9d63-5aa3-bbc5-ce166b76e68e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060422 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186059154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060422 #edb/ref 1069 "q-82626" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060422 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the converse of the following statement:\n\n**If** you can play in the garden, **then** it is not raining." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060422 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e5b5272-201a-501b-94d1-5bfb86524a1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060557 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060557 #edb/ref 1061 143402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060557 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "359b59a3-ea7e-534e-bcef-10e9ba22f6e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060557 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060558 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060558 #edb/ref 1061 143403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060558 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e2eb4f41-af42-517b-80cc-4e15c689de2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060558 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060559 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060559 #edb/ref 1061 143406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060559 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c85a942c-f9af-53b4-b1da-fa03e8854a2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060559 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060560 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060560 #edb/ref 1061 143409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060560 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a0cda17a-d96e-5ea8-bb9e-ccd79d7904dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060560 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060561 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060561 #edb/ref 1061 143412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060561 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74a00ea0-ef35-56ab-86b6-44c08bc91491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060561 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060562 #edb/ref 1061 143413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060562 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f375663a-5188-59fe-b4e2-7d315657f509" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060562 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060985 #edb/ref 1064 "A conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ has three related logical forms:\n\n- the **converse:** $B \\Rightarrow A$\n- the **inverse:** $\\lnot A \\Rightarrow \\lnot B$\n- the **contrapositive:** $\\lnot B \\Rightarrow \\lnot A$\n\nFor example, consider the following true statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ If I am George Washington, then I am an American.\n\nHere, the antecedent is $A=$ \"I am George Washington,\" and the consequent is $B=$ \"I am an American.\"\n\n- The *converse* is \"If I am an American, then I am George Washington.\"\n- The *inverse* is \"If I am not George Washington, then I am not an American.\"\n- The *contrapositive* is \"If I am not an American, then I am not George Washington.\"\n\nAs we can see from the statements above, only the contrapositive is also true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060985 #edb/ref 1065 7979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060985 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f381549b-ee22-54fc-8819-f65677237a08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060985 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060986 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, we have the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement: $$\\underbrace{A \\Rightarrow B}_{\\textrm{original}} \\equiv \\underbrace{\\neg B \\Rightarrow \\neg A}_{\\textrm{contrapositive}}$$\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other but not to the original statement: $$\\underbrace{\\neg A \\Rightarrow \\neg B}_{\\textrm{inverse}} \\equiv \\underbrace{B \\Rightarrow A}_{\\textrm{converse}}$$\n\nWe can prove these results using a truth table, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, A \\Rightarrow B \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\Rightarrow \\neg B \\,$ | $\\, B \\Rightarrow A \\,$ | $\\, \\neg B \\Rightarrow \\neg A \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n\nThis can be visualized using the following diagram.\n\n![](images/d9/d946d2a81d46cbde934ebbab54f9b69b514092e02da4f8a3b79768f218929449.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060986 #edb/ref 1065 7980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060986 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5bbcfb96-5707-521a-bea1-559cf154472f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060986 #edb/ref 1067 "Logically Equivalent Conditional Statements" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378320 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186378321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378320 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106796" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378320 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378320 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the contrapositive of the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ **If** it does not get warmer, **then** the sun does not come out." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378320 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6140dc58-ccf3-5a53-9ee2-21989ff9d18e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378320 #edb/ref 1074 "In our conditional statement: \n\n- The antecedent is $A:$ It does not get warmer.\n\n- The consequent is $B:$ The sun does not come out.\n\nThe contrapositive of a conditional statement $A⇒B$ is given by \n\n$$\n¬B⇒¬A.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the negations of $A$ and $B:$\n\n- $¬A:$ It gets warmer.\n\n- $¬B:$ The sun comes out.\n\nTherefore, the contrapositive is the following: \n\n$\\,$ If the sun comes out, then it gets warm." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378321 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378321 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378321 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378321 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378321 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378321 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378321 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378321 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186378325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378321 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d9f8ccbc-1687-5b4d-9590-26ca0dfa92bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378322 #edb/ref 1080 "If it gets warmer, then the sun comes out." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378322 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378322 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "91833904-266b-5225-a0db-02f970dbc0a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378323 #edb/ref 1080 "If the sun does not come out, then it gets warmer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378323 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378323 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "215eddd9-79f7-5359-b1cd-97569af0e2b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378324 #edb/ref 1080 "If the sun does not come out, then it does not get warmer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378324 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378324 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "df5b5f4e-faac-5af1-a95d-c12e7d26b374" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378325 #edb/ref 1080 "If the sun comes out, then it gets warmer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378325 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378325 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7397d158-4b17-51c3-bae5-1e3acf204602" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378326 #edb/ref 1080 "If the sun comes out, then it does not get warmer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378326 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378326 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "90d384b6-c07f-56e0-86ea-8f1bb7789e51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379281 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186379282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379281 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379281 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379281 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the following statement: \n\n$\\,$ **If** you are not sick, **then** you don't go to the hospital." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379281 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "be66b1f8-682e-59f1-a668-bd79f735e6c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186379281 #edb/ref 1074 "In our conditional statement:\n\n- The antecedent is $A:$ You are not sick.\n\n- The consequent is $B:$ You don't go to the hospital.\n\nThe inverse of a conditional statement $A⇒B$ is given by \n\n$$\n¬A⇒¬B.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the negation